OOP (Object Oriented Programming)
- aims to implement real-word entities, example: objects, classes, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism
- a methodology or paradigm for designing

Object
- any entity that has a state(data) and behavior(functionalities) is known as an object
- instance of a class
- can communicate without knowing details and data, important is the type of message accepted and message return

Elements of an object:
1. State: It is embodied in an object's properties. Additionally, it reflects an object's attributes.
2. Behavior: An object's methods are a representation of it. Additionally, it represents how an object
    reacts to other objects.
3. Identity: Identity is a special name that gives a thing the ability to communicate with other objects.
4. Method: A method is a group of statements that carry out a certain operation and give an outcome.
    A method can complete a certain task without giving back any results. Methods are regarded as time
    savers since they enable us to reuse the code without having to type it again. Java differs from languages like C, C++, and Python in that each method needs to be a component of a class.

*No class means no objects

Class
- is a logical entity -> does not consume space
- blueprint for an object

Class declaration includes:
1. Modifiers: public or default access
2. class keyword: keyword to create a class
3. class name: the name (capitalize by convention)
4. superclass (if any): The name of the class's parent (superclass), if any, preceded by the keyword extends. A class can only extend (subclass) one parent.
5. Interfaces (if any): A comma-separated list of interfaces implemented by the class, if any, preceded by the keyword implements. A class can implement more than one interface.
6. Body: The class body surrounded by braces, { }.

